::kind(api)

::meta{crate: tomet-semantics-core, dependencies: list(tomet-ast, tomet-type), dependents: list(tomet-config, tomet-declaration, tomet-semantics, tomet-validator-core)}

=[tomet-semantics-core]

==[tomet-semantics-core]

Shared core semantic model and type admission for Tomet and cotomet documents.

==[Module admits]

Type admission and conformance checking for AST [`Value`]s.

===[Structs]

====[struct Mismatch]

A value that is not of the type it was held to.

===[Functions]

====[fn admits]

Whether `value`, as a document holds it, is of this type.

A type that is text -- `string`, `version`, `uuid`, `nanoid`, and `datetime`
-- takes any scalar as the text it was written as, so a bare `2024` is a
`string` and a bare `1.10` is a `version`: what is written decides, not
what the reader would have made of it. A number type takes the numbers
the reader keeps as written too (`1.10` is a `float`, `-0` an `int`).

==[Module parse]

Parsing of type specifications from AST [`Value`]s.

===[Functions]

====[fn parse_type_spec]

Reads a `type:` specification as it is written in an AST [`Value`].

Accepts a bare name (`string`, `list`, `datetime`) as a string, a parameterized
call in AST form (`list(...)` which the parser resolves to [`Value::Seq`] or
leaves as [`Value::Call`]), or a string representation of a parameterized type
(such as `"list(string)"`).

==[Module positional]

Giving positional arguments their names.

The reader leaves a positional argument unnamed (an entry keyed [`POSITIONAL_ENTRY_KEY`]);
what the first, the second... are called is a signature, and the signature belongs to whoever
owns the element: a declaration's to `tomet-declaration`, a body element's to `tomet-semantics`.
The binding itself is the same for all of them, and is here, next to the key it undoes.

===[Functions]

====[fn bind_positional]

Names the positional arguments of `(args)` after `keys`, in order.

The reader keeps a positional argument as it was written: an entry whose key is
[`POSITIONAL_ENTRY_KEY`] in a map, an item of a sequence, or the lone scalar of `(x)`. A
signature says what the first, the second... are called; this puts the names on.

- A map: each remaining positional entry takes the next slot in `keys` that is not already

a real key, left to right on both sides. A slot with no positional entry left, and a positional
entry past the last slot, are both left as they are -- nothing is dropped, and an unassigned
entry keeps its empty key.

- A sequence (`(list(a, b))`): zipped against the slots by position; items past the last slot

are dropped.

- Any other value: the lone argument, under the first slot.

With no `keys` -- an element that has no positional slots -- `args` comes back unchanged.

This is the mechanism only; the signatures are the callers'.

===[Constants]

====[const POSITIONAL_ENTRY_KEY]

Sentinel key for a positional (unkeyed) entry inside a map body or args.
